# terraform.tfvars IS committed in this repository, deliberately.
#
# The values in them - account ids, the platform KMS ARN - are already in the
# tree in plain text: bootstrap/main.tf hardcodes them in its backend block,
# because a backend block cannot take variables. Gitignoring the tfvars hid
# nothing and cost a GitHub secret per layer to work around.
#
# The bar for anything added to a tfvars from here on is therefore: it must be
# publishable. An email address, a hostname, a token or a licence key does not
# go in one - use a TF_VAR_ environment variable or a secret for those.
#
# EXCEPT these four. They carry alert_emails - real addresses, which a public
# repository turns into permanent harvestable text. They stay out of the tree
# until the alerts move to a role alias on a domain that actually receives mail;
# CI falls back to TFVARS_<LAYER> for them. Listed by name rather than by pattern
# so that adding a layer does not silently re-ignore its tfvars.
bootstrap/terraform.tfvars
budgets/terraform.tfvars
organization/terraform.tfvars
identity/terraform.tfvars

# Never commit - the security workflow also blocks these at PR time.
.terraform/
# .terraform.lock.hcl is intentionally committed - it pins provider hashes
terraform.tfstate
terraform.tfstate.*
*.tfstate.backup
# A saved plan carries the full resource payload, sensitive values included.
tf.plan
*.tfplan
crash.log
override.tf
override.tf.json
*_override.tf
.terraformrc
terraform.rc
kubeconfig
*.pem
*.key
.env
